uid "660e9efe-0c2c-57ea-8c8f-8be8f8b69eca" :question/math-academy-id "q-106936" :question/problem "The rate at which a pot of hot oil cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the liquid in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${30}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the oil? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={30}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-30).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-106936/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106936"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-106937/selection/a723a97cc3c16502bb1ec8f44e87ebf3f6701a068ba51e51ddfb509a33c1dfa9" :answer/id #uuid "a59d0edc-f329-5321-ac62-4cc081be745c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-75)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106937/selection/761662688fb9d5d8b325f5af9fb70e40ed0ad2cb1a2f015b14ffb44099545ef6" :answer/id #uuid "976ac44e-a8b2-5b1e-8351-669cd0708f87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T+75)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106937/selection/7926e101682fa78e5058162bb49f89a8f66a4498af1f260267a830d14cf22b2b" :answer/id #uuid "e9bd00ed-8bf5-5ac