nstant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={75}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-75).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-106937/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106937"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-107122/selection/6514e4156c529d7305c2ffd484c350083b1fa24eec34fecbc47f56dfe28989b7" :answer/id #uuid "5cff09bc-54c0-5019-b5a3-eacc817ec533" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107122/selection/f3b66536ac73ea784c2a569aeae39de9581b63e966b70fdea5b9611b869fdc95" :answer/id #uuid "bf4c9b83-6249-51da-83b1-e34194030378" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11\\,\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107122/selection/e2ec641807538dbc47974d8d0ab5e4c29429514df6bcb04585a57fc9bdfb0d72" :answer/id #uuid "87ce2df6-7e54-5787-887f-6b705d17ad87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\,\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107122/selection/64c2838ecbd7a2939c2e5d441810abced34e0dfdd657a2ea53bdb38e0c8bdabd" :answer/id #uuid "efd0d021-fbc2-5be3-bcd6-b1419ffa6f63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107122/selection/d8e5ee21a358be9d5938f2b32594e8406c9346f6b15fc305eac71cb6e06720db" :answer/id #uuid "2aab88d7-1299-59f7-ae16-e915ede32a0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\,\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107122/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "818b37e4-1fc2-5e67-ba30-6d86a0240800" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-107122/selection/e2ec641807538dbc47974d8d0ab5e4c29429514df6bcb04585a57fc9bdfb0d72" :answer-field/choices ["q-107122/selection/6514e4156c529d7305c2ffd484c350083b1fa24eec34fecbc47f56dfe28989b7" "q-107122/selection/f3b66536ac73ea784c2a569aeae39de9581b63e966b70fdea5b9611b869fdc95" "q-107122/selection/e2ec641807538dbc47974d8d0ab5e4c29429514df6bcb04585a57fc9bdfb0d72" "q-107122/selection/64c2838ecbd7a2939c2e5d441810abced34e0dfdd657a2ea53bdb38e0c8bdabd" "q-107122/selection/d8e5ee21a358be9d5938f2b32594e8406c9346f6b15fc305eac71cb6e06720db"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-107122" :question/id #uuid "357c35a0-f2c3-51a5-ba26-cd42dadcc723" :question/math-academy-id "q-107122" :question/problem "A thermometer is taken out of a room where the air temperature is ${72}^{∘}F$  and brought outside where the temperature is ${15}^{∘}F,$ and its reading begins to decrease. After $2$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${54}^{∘}F.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the thermometer to register a reading of ${25}^{∘}F?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the thermometer was transferred outside to ${15}^{∘}F.$*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={15}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-15).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-15} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-15} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-15| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-15>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-15 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-15 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-15 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =15+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${72}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=72,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}72 & =15+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 72 & =15+K \\\\ K & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=15+57{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $2$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${54}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(2)=54,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}54 & =15+57{e}^{-2k} \\\\ 39 & =57{e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\frac{39}{57} & ={e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{57}) & =-2k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{57})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the thermometer reads ${25}^{∘}F.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =25 \\\\ 15+57{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =25 \\\\ 57{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =10 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{10}{57} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{57}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{57}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the thermometer will read ${25}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{57})}{-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{57})}≈9\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-107122/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-107122"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
